ng this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, local isometries carries the completed idea into normal fields and shape operator."}]}, {"id": "aa742a0c-2bfa-413c-93da-13e38ac19568", "type": "topic", "title": "Normal fields and shape operator", "description": "This module builds directly on first fundamental form and develops normal fields and shape operator. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for second fundamental form.", "children": [{"id": "42ba14d8-d9b8-41c3-8558-eda085c1759c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gauss map", "children": [], "description": "Gauss map gives normal fields and shape operator a reusable representation for organizing assumptions, variables, and relationships before analysis begins. It appears before normal curvature because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "c7a99d32-841f-4a11-9d2d-7461efe6a226", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Normal curvature", "children": [], "description": "Normal curvature is a governing quantity in normal fields and shape operator, linking local calculations to system-level behavior and offering a consistency check. After gauss map, normal curvature supplies what is needed to address principal directions."}, {"id": "7484c6b0-1ab1-468d-bf32-5e933a6ad455", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Principal directions", "children": [], "description": "Principal directions identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within normal fields and shape operator. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, principal directions carries the completed idea into second fundamental form."}]}, {"id": "b37dda01-3c6f-4268-a793-7e9833cbe96b", "type": "topic", "title": "Second fundamental form", "description": "This module builds directly on normal fields and shape operator and develops second fundamental form. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for geodesics.", "children": [{"id": "99e87d73-52aa-4ca8-a99c-cba0515600e9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Principal/Gaussian/mean curvature", "children": [], "description": "Principal/Gaussian/mean curvature is a governing quantity in second fundamental form, linking local calculations to system-level behavior and offering a consistency check. It appears before classification of points because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "52ea40f4-b729-4b35-b7b8-51b0b054a593", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classification of points", "children": [], "description": "Classification of points identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within second fundamental form. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, classification of points carries the completed idea into geodesics."}]}, {"id": "080fb534-7507-4cd8-813f-14252bd7605d", "type": "topic", "title": "Geodesics", "description": "This module builds directly on second fundamental form and develops geodesics. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for intrinsic geometry.", "children": [{"id": "461900e9-5e0d-41f2-b334-aebe0d214eee", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Covariant derivative", "children": [], "description": "Covariant derivative identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within geodesics. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. It appears before geodesic equations because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "067048cd-76bc-4fa5-8ee3-8bd268c253d1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Geodesic equations", "children": [], "description": "Geodesic equations identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within geodesics. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After covariant derivative, geodesic equations supplies what is needed to address exponential-map intuition."}, {"id": "2e43b657-e9f8-4fb3-b854-f8ce70165118", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Exponential-map intuition", "children": [], "description": "Exponential-map intuition gives geodesics a reusable representation for organizing assumptions, variables, and relationships before analysis begins. As the final topic in this module, exponential-map intuition carries the completed idea into intrinsic geometry."}]}, {"id": "69d8c0c4-07b6-4d1d-8a34-1b9ccd4b37c2", "type": "topic", "title": "Intrinsic geometry", "description": "This module builds directly on geodesics and develops intrinsic geometry. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for gauss–bonnet.", "children": [{"id": "14f213b0-ef4e-41bb-a878-dbf2061cc9e4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Christoffel symbols", "children": [], "description": "Christoffel symbols identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within intrinsic geometry. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. It appears before theorema egregium because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "b22f1fd6-fa3b-4cd2-8745-2c24c4de182b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theorema Egregium", "children": [], "description": "Theorema Egregium provides a formal guarantee or inference rule for intrinsic geometry, allowing later conclusions to be justified rather than inferred from examples alone. After christoffel symbols, theorema egregium supplies what is needed to address isometries."}, {"id": "ed1606ee-3a13-42cb-8347-067168b10c3d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Isometries", "children": [], "description": "Isometries identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within intrinsic geometry. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. As the final topic in this module, isometries carries the completed idea into gauss–bonnet."}]}, {"id": "d5f5365a-685f-46d3-9e8e-ee1fce1d8fdc", "type": "topic", "title": "Gauss–Bonnet", "description": "This module builds directly on intrinsic geometry and develops gauss–bonnet. It belongs here because its methods and distinctions are prerequisites for synthesis and extensions.", "children": [{"id": "6ff3a110-7b9b-4aa6-b202-486335e3473e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Geodesic curvature", "children": [], "description": "Geodesic curvature is a governing quantity in gauss–bonnet, linking local calculations to system-level behavior and offering a consistency check. It appears before local theorem because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "ddac7fe7-8f15-4d4f-99e6-6726f9d285ee", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Local theorem", "children": [], "description": "Local theorem provides a formal guarantee or inference rule for gauss–bonnet, allowing later conclusions to be justified rather than inferred from examples alone. After geodesic curvature, local theorem supplies what is needed to address global/topological consequences."}, {"id": "440d2f4e-b93e-455d-ab83-d1b55478e19a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global/topological consequences", "children": [], "description": "Global/topological consequences provides a formal guarantee or inference rule for gauss–bonnet, allowing later conclusions to be justified rather than inferred from examples alone. As the final topic in this module, global/topological consequences carries the completed idea into synthesis and extensions."}]}, {"id": "9d93f933-8ceb-49b9-81c2-37d3fb611f79", "type": "topic", "title": "Synthesis and extensions", "description": "This culminating module draws on gauss–bonnet and develops synthesis and extensions. It belongs last because it requires the earlier tools to be selected, combined, and evaluated rather than used one at a time.", "children": [{"id": "f5293b3e-2d39-4ecd-a37b-6db8779f6055", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Minimal surfaces", "children": [], "description": "Minimal surfaces identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within synthesis and extensions. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. It appears before manifolds because that next topic assumes this starting point."}, {"id": "4fb6df58-60cd-40d6-b84d-741d9c385589", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Manifolds", "children": [], "description": "Manifolds identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within synthesis and extensions. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After minimal surfaces, manifolds supplies what is needed to address forms."}, {"id": "a611d078-c222-4e16-9508-9bd3b4596d75", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Forms", "children": [], "description": "Forms identifies a specific object, distinction, or relationship within synthesis and extensions. Learners need to recognize and use it before combining this module's ideas in later work. After manifolds, forms supplies what is needed to address computational geometry."}, {"id": "14b20e70-e1e3-4a3c-9620-0fbe88975728", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Computational geometry", "children": [], "description": "Computational geometry is an executable part of synthesis and extensions; its correctness, cost, and failure modes recur in later problems and implementations. As the final course topic, computational geometry requires ideas from the complete path to be integrated and transferred."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$diffgeozzjs$::jsonb,
